WEBVTT

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:30.815
This is Threshold and Bar for the week to Wednesday the eleventh of November. It is a full week since episode ten, and the verified material runs to about forty minutes, under the usual hour, so this one is shorter rather than padded.

00:00:30.815 --> 00:00:50.890
The main items: the reported zone two U-turn, and the case on each side of it, including a new meta-analysis on training intensity and thresholds. What Andreas Almgren's breath tests showed about carbohydrate he drank against carbohydrate he burned. A Barbell Medicine reading of the studies cited in Andrew Huberman's new book.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:01:04.290
A forty-week trial of lifting during tirzepatide treatment. Records ratified, Olympic marathon standards set, and the fastest Ironman on record. And a German broadcaster's estimate of doping among children.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:13.315
There is no Equipment segment this week, because Gluck's Gym published no new review since the last episode, and nothing from the reading pile was in scope.

00:01:19.495 --> 00:01:37.270
The Boston Half on Sunday the eighth produced the first sub-hour half marathon run in Massachusetts. Habtom Samuel of Eritrea broke away shortly after fifteen kilometres and ran the last four miles alone to finish in fifty-nine minutes forty-nine seconds, according to the Boston Athletic Association.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:52.520
That took forty-five seconds off the event record of one hour, thirty-four seconds, set thirteen years ago by Lelisa Desisa. Andrea Kiptoo was second in one hour, one minute and three seconds, Abel Kipchumba third in one hour, one minute and sixteen.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:02:06.770
Jack Aho was sixth and top American in one hour, one minute and fifty-three seconds, which the B A A lists as the fastest by an American in the event's history. Samuel said afterwards that he knew at ten kilometres they were on for the record.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:13.545
Fentaye Belayneh of Ethiopia became the first woman to win the Boston Half three years running.

00:02:13.545 --> 00:02:32.645
She went through five kilometres in sixteen thirty-eight and ten in thirty-three oh two, sat in the pack over the rolling course, and outsprinted Winfred Mbithe by two seconds, one hour, nine minutes and fourteen seconds to one hour, nine minutes and sixteen. Daniel Romanchuk set an event record in the men's wheelchair race.

00:02:33.295 --> 00:02:44.120
In triathlon, Magnus Ditlev of Denmark won Ironman Florida in Panama City Beach on Saturday the seventh in seven hours, nineteen minutes and seventeen seconds.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:03:08.445
Slowtwitch's report gives his splits as forty-seven fifty-two for the swim, three hours, fifty-one minutes and sixteen seconds on the bike, and two hours, thirty-three minutes and thirty-one seconds for the marathon, and says the time is the fastest ever at an Ironman-branded race, ahead of Kristian Blummenfelt's seven twenty-one twelve at Cozumel, and quicker than the seven twenty-one oh four Sam Laidlow set at Challenge Roth this year.

00:03:08.445 --> 00:03:28.220
The bike split is also reported as a full-distance best. Rudy Von Berg was second and Matt Hanson third, with a two hours, twenty-eight minute marathon. Alanis Siffert of Switzerland won the women's race. A caution, which is my own: Slowtwitch's headline calls this a world record, but long-course triathlon has no ratified records.

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:35.120
Courses, currents and conditions differ, so a world best is a fair description and a world record is not.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:53.545
World Athletics ratified four world records on Monday the ninth. Two are in our scope. Josh Kerr's mile of three minutes, forty-two point six six seconds in London on the eighteenth of July, which took the record from Hicham El Guerrouj's three forty-three point one three, set in Rome in nineteen ninety-nine.

00:03:53.545 --> 00:04:07.420
World Athletics gives Kerr's splits as fifty-five point three at four hundred metres, one fifty-one point one at eight hundred, and three twenty-seven point six two at fifteen hundred, faster than his own British record for that distance.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:22.945
And Yomif Kejelcha's half marathon of fifty-six minutes and fifty-one seconds in Buenos Aires on the twenty-third of August, twenty-nine seconds under Jacob Kiplimo's previous record. His second ten kilometres took twenty-six minutes and forty-five seconds.

00:04:22.945 --> 00:04:34.645
The other two are Alison dos Santos's forty-five point eight oh in the four hundred metres hurdles and Masai Russell's twelve point oh nine in the hundred metres hurdles, both in Zurich.

00:04:35.295 --> 00:04:55.045
A day later, World Athletics published the qualification system for the Los Angeles Olympics in twenty twenty-eight. The marathon entry standards are two hours and five minutes for men and two hours, twenty-two minutes and twenty seconds for women. The marathon window opened on the first of November and runs to the end of April twenty twenty-eight.

00:04:55.045 --> 00:05:06.070
The split between the two routes in has changed: about forty percent of athletes are expected to qualify by standard and sixty percent through world rankings, where it used to be half and half.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:19.370
In the marathon, anyone ranked above fifty-sixth on the Road to L A list on the nineteenth of March twenty twenty-eight, at three per country, is in, and a top-five finish at a Platinum Label marathon counts as the standard.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:39.295
World Athletics explains the fast marathon standards with a line worth quoting: "in an Olympic year, times are between thirty to sixty seconds faster than the trends would forecast." A national Olympic committee can still reallocate a place to an athlete who has run two hours eleven, or two hours twenty-nine for women, inside the window.

00:05:45.475 --> 00:05:55.500
Kenya's anti-doping agency, ADAK, has provisionally suspended Mercy Oketch, the national four hundred metres record holder indoors and out.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:13.725
Marathon Handbook, reading ADAK's list, reports two substances: trimetazidine, a heart drug prescribed for angina that WADA classes as a metabolic modulator, and furosemide, a diuretic on the list as a masking agent. The entry is dated the twenty-sixth of October.

00:06:13.725 --> 00:06:30.950
The list gives no test date and does not say whether the sample was taken in or out of competition. Oketch is twenty-four, set her outdoor record of fifty point one four in Nairobi in May twenty twenty-five, and was fifth in the World Indoor final in March, the first Kenyan woman to reach it.

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:44.000
As with every case on this programme, a provisional suspension is not a finding. Trimetazidine is a non-specified substance, which under World Athletics rules makes the suspension mandatory while the case is heard.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:07:02.050
The same agency issued a correction that deserves more attention than it will get. On the fifth of November ADAK said the athlete suspended from the eighth of October for the glucocorticoid triamcinolone acetonide is Cherokony Noah Kibet, who races three thousand metres and longer.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:15.175
It is not Noah Kibet, the eight hundred metres runner and twenty twenty-two World Indoor silver medallist, whose name and photograph had circulated with the story. ADAK asked outlets to correct their reports.

00:07:15.175 --> 00:07:23.550
The cost of a wrong name match falls on an athlete who did nothing, which is why the correction deserves the same reach as the original reports.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:38.525
The German broadcaster A R D published on Sunday the eighth the headline figure from a documentary series on child doping: at least ten million minors worldwide affected. The method is worth understanding before the number is repeated.

00:07:38.525 --> 00:08:00.775
A R D took the United Nations range of a hundred and ninety-five to two hundred and sixty million children aged eight to seventeen in competitive sport, used about two hundred million, and applied the average from forty-four anonymous surveys in fifteen countries, which put use of hard substances such as anabolic steroids and growth hormone at five to six percent.

00:08:00.775 --> 00:08:24.100
Paulo David, formerly secretary of the United Nations Committee on the Rights of the Child, told them: "there is a big, massive problem, and nothing is being done about it." Against that, WADA's own Operation Refuge report found one thousand four hundred and sixteen minors testing positive worldwide between twenty twelve and September twenty twenty-three, about a hundred and eighteen a year.

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:40.300
A R D itself says an exact figure cannot be determined and that the surveys differ in method, size and timing. My reading: the ten million is an extrapolation from self-report in fifteen countries to the whole world, so it is an order of magnitude, not a count.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:54.825
The more robust point is the gap between survey prevalence of several percent and about a hundred positive tests a year worldwide, and it does not depend on the extrapolation. The series is in the A R D Media Library from the thirteenth.

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:11.615
The largest piece of new evidence this week is a randomised trial in Nature Medicine, published on Monday the ninth, by Daniel Green, Bu Yeap and colleagues in Western Australia.

00:09:11.615 --> 00:09:31.065
A hundred and twenty adults with overweight or obesity were all treated with tirzepatide for forty weeks, and half were randomised to supervised resistance training three times a week. Adherence to the training was ninety-one percent. Weight loss was the same in both arms, about twenty-two and a half kilograms. Fat loss was the same.

00:09:31.065 --> 00:09:49.015
What differed was lean soft tissue: four point six seven kilograms lost on the drug alone against three point five seven with training, a difference of about one point one kilograms, with a confidence interval of zero point four to one point seven five. Strength diverged in direction, not just size.

00:09:49.015 --> 00:10:02.590
On the drug alone, bench press fell by about four kilograms, lat pulldown by four and a half and knee extension by four and a half. With training, they rose by four point seven, eight point six and five point nine kilograms.

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:22.615
The authors' own caveats: participants were middle-aged to older, initially sedentary, and without diabetes or cardiovascular disease, so the result may not generalise to everyone on the drug, and lean soft tissue by D X A is not the same as skeletal muscle. The finding that matters to anyone who lifts is the strength result.

00:10:22.615 --> 00:10:29.565
By my arithmetic, lean mass loss was cut by only about a quarter, but strength went up instead of down.

00:10:30.215 --> 00:10:50.115
A cohort study in Circulation, Population Health and Outcomes, published on the second of November, used the All of Us programme's linked Fitbit and health records for ten thousand three hundred and sixty-two American adults, median age fifty-three, seventy percent women, with at least ninety valid nights over a hundred and eighty days.

00:10:50.115 --> 00:10:59.340
Over a median of three point seven years, two hundred and thirty developed the composite of heart attack, heart failure, cardiomyopathy or stroke.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:20.765
Each extra twenty-nine minutes of night-to-night variability in sleep duration, one standard deviation, was associated with a hazard ratio of one point two four, confidence interval one point oh nine to one point four one. Variability in bedtime and wake time showed much the same. Mean sleep duration was not associated, at zero point nine six.

00:11:20.765 --> 00:11:39.290
This is observational, the event count is modest, and Fitbit staging is an estimate. The authors call their one novel finding, that more sleep cycles go with less atrial arrhythmia, one requiring confirmation. The plain reading is that regularity carried more signal than average duration in this sample.

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:54.240
The Santa Barbara Half Marathon on Sunday the eighth ended with thirty of seven thousand five hundred registered runners treated for symptoms consistent with heat illness, according to the county health department as reported by Edhat.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:12:11.890
Twenty-six went to emergency departments, four were treated at the scene, and three remained in hospital on Monday. The first call came at eight forty-one in the morning. Inspectors who had checked the race's electrolyte drink preparation on the Friday and Saturday found, in the county's words, "No findings".

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:28.665
The county health officer, Henning Ansorg, said the incident "appears to be an isolated event connected directly to race participation and the heat". Police are investigating, and there has been social media speculation about a water station that officials have not supported.

00:12:28.665 --> 00:12:40.340
The general lesson, which is mine and not the county's, is old: an unseasonably warm race day catches out people who trained in cool weather, and a later finish means more of the race in the heat.

00:12:46.530 --> 00:13:06.405
The running science this week was dominated by an argument rather than a paper. On The Real Science of Sport, the show notes summarise it as Peter Attia admitting that zone two is not needed for people training a couple of hours a week. I have not read or heard Attia's own words on this, so I will report the reaction, not characterise his position.

00:13:07.055 --> 00:13:22.580
The strongest version of the case against zone two as a public-health prescription is a narrative review published in Sports Medicine in twenty twenty-five, by Kristi Storoschuk, Martin Gibala, Brendon Gurd and a colleague, titled "Much Ado About Zone Two".

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:42.955
They argue that the popular recommendation rests largely on observational data from elite endurance athletes who do a lot of easy work and have high mitochondrial capacity, and they conclude that current evidence does not support zone two as the optimal intensity for improving mitochondrial or fat oxidative capacity.

00:13:42.955 --> 00:13:57.880
They add that higher intensities matter more for cardiometabolic health, particularly when total training volume is low. That last condition is the important one. Their target is the person training a few hours a week, not the runner doing eight hours.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:09.105
Steve Magness answered on Monday the ninth with a post titled "We Settled the Zone Two versus H I I T Debate in the Nineteen Fifties". His argument has three parts.

00:14:09.105 --> 00:14:26.780
First, that zones are rough categories; in his words, zone two is "a very rough idea to categorize 'pretty easy running.'" Second, that most training studies last six to ten weeks, and intensity works quickly while an aerobic base takes time, so short studies will tend to favour intensity.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:44.280
Third, that studies measure V O two max, economy and threshold when fresh, but not how well those hold up late in a long race. He criticises a twenty twenty-five review, which he does not name, for focusing narrowly on mitochondria, and my inference is that he means the Storoschuk paper.

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:54.830
His summary of what has survived a century of coaching is: "Lots of easy, some moderate, a bit of hard to sharpen up, and occasionally go see God to realign perspectives."

00:14:55.480 --> 00:15:07.005
These two positions are closer than the noise suggests. Storoschuk and colleagues are talking about the general public at low volume, and they are right that a few easy hours a week is not a magic dose.

00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:25.030
Magness is talking about people trying to run a race well, often at higher volume, and he concedes that at very low volume some easy running should become steady. Where they really disagree is on evidence. Magness weights coaching history and performance. The review weights controlled mechanistic studies, which are short.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:37.155
Neither fully answers the other, and that is a limit of the literature, not of either argument. Brady Holmer's own short response was that a man is allowed to change his mind when presented with newer data.

00:15:37.805 --> 00:15:54.430
A new meta-analysis bears on a related question: where to train relative to a threshold in order to move it. Ratko Peric and colleagues, in PeerJ on the twenty-second of October, pooled thirteen studies, twenty-four training groups and three hundred and thirty-eight participants.

00:15:54.430 --> 00:16:11.255
Training intensity relative to the threshold was the most consistent moderator. For the aerobic threshold, supra-threshold training improved it by thirty-eight point seven percent on average, at-threshold training by fourteen point seven, and sub-threshold by eighteen point four.

00:16:11.255 --> 00:16:25.605
For the anaerobic threshold, the graded pattern was two point six percent below, about seven at, and twelve point two above. On its face, this cuts against sub-threshold approaches. The caveats are large, and the authors state most of them.

00:16:25.605 --> 00:16:41.055
Heterogeneity was eighty-nine percent for the aerobic threshold, and the prediction interval ran from minus two to plus forty-nine percent. Lactate-based thresholds produced larger effects than ventilatory ones. The participants were untrained or amateur.

00:16:41.055 --> 00:16:59.380
And the authors write that chronic work near threshold "raises concerns about long-term effectiveness and non-functional overreaching in elite athletes", and that, untested, the best intensity may differ with fitness. The strongest case for this paper is that in short studies, harder sessions move thresholds more.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:12.580
The strongest case for sub-threshold training, as in the Norwegian model, is that it lets trained athletes accumulate far more threshold-adjacent work across years without breaking down. These studies do not test that.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:26.135
Two studies this week asked what happens late in a hard run, and one asked whether cold water helps you come back the next day.

00:17:26.785 --> 00:17:46.410
Ben Hunter, Ed Maunder and colleagues, in the Scandinavian Journal of Medicine and Science in Sports, had ten trained runners, seven men and three women, average age thirty-eight, do incremental tests fresh and again after ninety minutes of running, at nineteen degrees and at thirty degrees, both at forty percent humidity.

00:17:46.410 --> 00:18:05.810
Fresh, the speed at the first ventilatory threshold was about seven percent lower in the heat, roughly zero point eight kilometres an hour. After ninety minutes it fell by eight point six percent in the cool and eleven point one in the warm, with no statistical interaction, so the heat did not accelerate the decline of the threshold itself.

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:17.835
But running economy drifted more in the heat, by five point four percent from fifteen to ninety minutes against one point seven, and end-test speed fell by twelve point one percent against six point eight.

00:18:17.835 --> 00:18:37.535
The authors' summary is that after prolonged running in the warm, threshold speed sat on average seventeen point one percent below its fresh value in the cool. That number is the practical one. A runner who sets easy or threshold pace on a cool day and then runs long in the heat may be in a different intensity domain from the one intended.

00:18:37.535 --> 00:18:45.435
Ten runners, one humidity level, and intensity prescribed relative to each condition, so treat the detail cautiously.

00:18:46.085 --> 00:18:53.410
A related heat paper came up on the Some Work, All Play podcast this week, which David and Megan Roche discussed.

00:18:53.410 --> 00:19:14.685
Peter McDonald, Julien Périard and colleagues, in Medicine and Science in Sports and Exercise, had trained male cyclists do eight days of heat acclimation either in dry heat or in humid heat matched for wet-bulb globe temperature, then a thirty-minute time trial in both conditions. Both kinds of acclimation improved performance in both environments.

00:19:14.685 --> 00:19:27.935
In the dry time trial, dry acclimation added fourteen watts against six for humid, a further eight watts. In the humid time trial, the gains were similar, nine and twelve watts. Groups were small, ten and twelve men.

00:19:27.935 --> 00:19:38.160
For a runner preparing for a humid race without access to humidity, the practical reading is that ordinary heat training transfers, which was also the hosts' conclusion.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:47.910
Brady Holmer, writing for Marathon Handbook, covered a small trial by Paula Brozinga and colleagues in the International Journal of Sports Medicine.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:20:07.160
Seventeen recreational male runners did an eleven-day block, two five-day blocks with a rest day between, each session thirty minutes of continuous running, one maximal three hundred metres and ten more three hundred metre intervals. Afterwards, one group sat in ten degree water for fifteen minutes, the other sat and rested.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:23.685
Soreness and blood markers of muscle damage rose. Three hundred metre times did not change in either group, and cold water made no difference to performance, soreness, creatine kinase or perceived effort. It is a small study, all men, and assignment details are not in the abstract.

00:20:23.685 --> 00:20:34.410
Holmer's practical suggestion is a good one: during a hard block, record both how sore the legs feel and a repeatable performance, because in this study they told different stories.

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:52.010
The same day, Holmer covered a cross-sectional study from Kexin Fang and colleagues in Frontiers in Physiology, of a hundred and forty-one men aged twenty-eight to forty living in a cold region, split at the group's median three thousand metre time of fourteen minutes thirteen seconds.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:21:11.085
After adjusting for limb muscle mass and correcting for multiple comparisons, ten of six hundred and thirty-six serum metabolites differed between faster and slower men, including higher cortisol in the faster group, and no metabolic pathway survived correction. There was no measure of diet or V O two max.

00:21:11.085 --> 00:21:21.885
Holmer's question for anyone selling a performance blood panel is the right one: when people change training or diet on the result, do their times improve? This study cannot say.

00:21:28.065 --> 00:21:42.165
Last week we covered the training behind Andreas Almgren's New York debut. This week Maurten, his sponsor, published the carbohydrate data from his build, on a page that also sells the products, and Marathon Handbook reported it.

00:21:42.165 --> 00:21:56.690
On two long runs in August and September he drank thirty-six grams of carbohydrate every fifteen minutes from a drink containing a naturally occurring carbon thirteen tracer, and breath samples tracked how much of it he oxidised.

00:21:56.690 --> 00:22:12.790
On the first, a thirty-seven kilometre run at an average of three fifteen per kilometre, the drink supplied three percent of the carbohydrate he burned at five kilometres, ten percent at ten, about a quarter from sixteen kilometres, and twenty-nine percent at thirty.

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:26.590
The peak oxidation rate from the drink was one point four grams a minute, eighty-three grams an hour. Maurten estimated his total carbohydrate use at two hundred and twenty-two grams an hour, so stored glycogen did most of the work.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:37.440
Across both runs, the drink never contributed more than eighty-seven grams an hour, against an intake Marathon Handbook puts at a hundred and forty-four on his hardest runs.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:54.865
In Almgren's words, "You can't store enough carbohydrates to be able to utilize everything for the entire workout, you need extra fueling." Maurten's model said that without fuel he would run short at marathon pace after about eighty minutes. In New York he planned about a hundred and twenty grams an hour.

00:22:55.515 --> 00:23:15.040
On Some Work, All Play, David and Megan Roche discussed the same data and the wider move to high carbohydrate intake among elite endurance athletes. This is one athlete, tested by a company with a commercial interest, and the results of a third test at altitude were still being analysed. But it fits the controlled evidence.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:28.790
Tim Podlogar and colleagues, in the European Journal of Applied Physiology in twenty twenty-two, gave eleven trained male cyclists either a hundred and twenty or ninety grams an hour during three hours at moderate intensity.

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:49.190
Exogenous oxidation in the last hour was higher with the bigger dose, one point five one against one point two nine grams a minute, but oxidation of the body's own stores did not differ. More carbohydrate in meant more carbohydrate burned from the bottle, not more glycogen spared. Two practical points follow, and both are labelled as my inference.

00:23:49.190 --> 00:24:08.515
First, the lag: with the drink contributing little for the first forty minutes or so, fuelling from the start of a long effort makes sense, which is also Maurten's own advice. Second, intake above what can be oxidised has to go somewhere, which is why gut tolerance is the usual limit, and Almgren said he had no stomach problems.

00:24:09.165 --> 00:24:21.140
Brady Holmer's Physiology Friday covered the other end of carbohydrate availability: a randomised crossover trial by Kristine Lange, Jørn Helge and colleagues in the Journal of Physiology.

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:38.640
Ten trained men did a long glycogen-depleting session, then ate either high or low carbohydrate for the rest of the day, and were retested the next morning with muscle biopsies. Peak fat oxidation rose after both, by twenty-four percent after high carbohydrate and ninety percent after low.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:58.765
The intensity at which fat oxidation peaked moved only after the low-carbohydrate day, from forty-one to fifty-six percent of V O two peak. Maximal power was about seven percent lower, and effort felt harder. And the mitochondria did not change: coupling control and efficiency were the same in both arms. Holmer's line captures it.

00:24:58.765 --> 00:25:15.265
A higher fat oxidation reading after a low-glycogen day is "a snapshot of fuel partitioning", not "a change in cellular machinery". The practical point for anyone who sets training zones from a lab fat-max test is that the result depends on what was eaten the day before.

00:25:15.265 --> 00:25:21.440
Ten men, two days, so this is about acute fuel selection, not long-term adaptation.

00:25:22.090 --> 00:25:38.040
A systematic review and meta-analysis from Loughborough, by Freya Gibbons, Stephen Mears and colleagues, published in Sports Medicine on Tuesday the tenth, asked whether adding protein to a drink helps hydration. Sixteen studies, two hundred and five participants.

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:57.465
In rehydration studies, protein-containing drinks increased fluid retention with a standardised mean difference of zero point six six, reduced urine output by zero point seven one, improved net fluid balance by zero point five eight, and increased plasma volume change by a smaller zero point two nine.

00:25:57.465 --> 00:26:14.015
Mean protein content was about twenty-four grams per litre. The authors flag two limits: only seventeen percent of participants were women, and there were no plant-based drinks, which they say limits generalisation to female athletes and to people whose diets exclude dairy.

00:26:14.015 --> 00:26:23.715
The mechanism is plausible, through slower gastric emptying and oncotic pressure, but these are short laboratory studies of fluid balance, not performance.

00:26:24.365 --> 00:26:41.240
Last in fuelling, a meta-analysis in P LOS One by Jinfa Gu and colleagues pooled nine studies of vitamin D supplementation in three hundred and twelve football and futsal players. Supplementation raised blood levels substantially, with a Hedges' g of one point four one.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:56.190
It did nothing measurable for jump, sprint, aerobic capacity or strength, with all confidence intervals crossing zero, and the certainty was low to very low. The abstract reports no analysis by baseline status, season or latitude.

00:26:56.190 --> 00:27:04.440
Correcting a deficiency is a health question in its own right, and this paper says it is not a performance aid in already-trained players.

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:29.230
Barbell Medicine's podcast this week, published on Wednesday the eleventh, is a review of Andrew Huberman's new book, Protocols. Jordan Feigenbaum read the book and pulled the studies it cites for the protocols that overlap with their work, and Austin Baraki guessed what the book recommends before hearing what the citations found.

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:40.980
The show notes stress that they are rating what the book tells people to do, not Huberman personally. I have not read the book, so everything about its contents here is as Feigenbaum reads it on the podcast.

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:59.930
The lifting protocol, as Feigenbaum quotes it, is to train each major muscle group directly only once a week, with four to eight hard sets, and the book says of it, according to the show notes, "I've seen it work for everyone I've suggested it to." Feigenbaum's point is that the book's own citations point the other way.

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:08.505
One is a twenty twenty-two systematic review by Eneko Baz-Valle and colleagues in the Journal of Human Kinetics, which I have checked.

00:28:08.505 --> 00:28:27.905
It pooled six randomised trials in trained young men and concluded that "a range of twelve to twenty weekly sets per muscle group may be an optimum standard recommendation" for hypertrophy, with no difference between moderate and high volume for the quadriceps or biceps and an advantage for higher volume in the triceps.

00:28:27.905 --> 00:28:32.955
That is a small review, but it does not support four to eight sets as optimal.

00:28:33.605 --> 00:28:45.080
The one study cited for once-a-week training is a twenty twenty-four trial by Helene Pedersen, Brad Schoenfeld, Vidar Andersen and colleagues in the European Journal of Sport Science.

00:28:45.080 --> 00:29:04.580
Twenty-two resistance-trained adults did the same weekly lower-body work, four multi-joint exercises at four to five sets each, either in one session or spread over four. After eight weeks, squat one-rep max rose by fifteen kilograms with four sessions against eight with one, a significant difference of seven kilograms.

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:21.730
Muscle thickness and jump height improved in both groups with no difference between them. So the study supports once a week as viable for hypertrophy and argues against it for strength. Here is Jordan Feigenbaum on the Barbell Medicine podcast, giving his general reading of the frequency literature.

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:34.880
[quoted clip]

00:29:36.222 --> 00:29:51.122
That is a fair summary of the dose-response meta-analyses, with one qualification of my own: Pedersen's strength result suggests frequency can matter beyond volume for a skill-heavy lift like the squat, perhaps through practice and session quality.

00:29:51.122 --> 00:30:01.197
The book also concedes, Feigenbaum says, that stopping a rep or two short of failure still works, citing this year's American College of Sports Medicine position stand.

00:30:01.847 --> 00:30:18.797
The cold plunge section produced a disagreement worth showing. The book cites a study of winter swimmers for a claim about brown fat. That study, by Susanna Søberg and colleagues in Cell Reports Medicine in twenty twenty-one, compared about eight young men who winter swim with eight who do not.

00:30:18.797 --> 00:30:34.597
Feigenbaum points out that it measured brown fat glucose uptake on a P E T scan, which shows activity rather than amount, at one time point, with no follow-up, and the abstract reports that the swimmers showed no brown fat activity at a comfortable temperature.

00:30:34.597 --> 00:30:53.897
On the wider evidence, he quotes a twenty twenty-five meta-analysis as concluding that the purported benefits remain unsubstantiated. I opened that paper's abstract, by Tara Cain and colleagues in P LOS One, and it reads more mixed than that. Eleven studies, three thousand one hundred and seventy-seven participants.

00:30:53.897 --> 00:31:11.222
Inflammation rose immediately after immersion. Stress fell twelve hours afterwards but not at the other time points. A narrative synthesis found improved sleep quality and quality of life, not mood, and a twenty-nine percent reduction in sickness absence among people taking cold showers.

00:31:11.222 --> 00:31:30.672
The authors call the evidence base constrained by few trials, small samples and a lack of diversity. I could not find the phrase he quotes in the abstract; it may be in the full text. Both readings agree that the benefits are modest and uncertain. Feigenbaum's own bottom line on cold plunges was: if you like it, go for it.

00:31:36.862 --> 00:31:42.662
Three papers this week for anyone combining lifting with running, or coaching people who do.

00:31:43.312 --> 00:32:03.712
A systematic review in Applied Physiology, Nutrition, and Metabolism by Nathalia Dias, Cleiton Libardi and colleagues counted who takes part in hypertrophy research. Of five hundred and seventy resistance training studies, seventy-two point two percent recruited only men, eleven percent only women, and sixteen point eight percent both.

00:32:03.712 --> 00:32:18.137
Among studies that included women, eight point five percent reported or accounted for menstrual cycle phase. Molecular analyses were nearly four times as common in men-only studies. There was modest improvement after twenty twenty.

00:32:18.137 --> 00:32:28.262
The authors note that relative hypertrophy responses look broadly similar between sexes, but the evidence base that coaching advice rests on is largely male.

00:32:28.912 --> 00:32:47.237
A biomechanics study in the Journal of Applied Physiology by Kolja Weidlich, Adamantios Arampatzis and colleagues in Berlin measured Achilles tendon strain in eighteen adolescent athletes during four common plyometric jumps: countermovement, drop, hurdle and horizontal single-leg jumps.

00:32:47.237 --> 00:33:05.487
Although ankle moments differed between jumps, peak tendon strain did not, averaging nine to ten point seven percent, above the strain range the authors cite as effective for tendon adaptation. Their conclusion is that frequent maximal-effort plyometrics may contribute to Achilles overload in adolescents.

00:33:05.487 --> 00:33:24.987
My inference for adult runners who add jumps to their strength work is narrower: if all maximal jumps load the tendon similarly, choosing a gentler-looking jump does not reduce tendon strain much; volume and effort are the parameters to adjust. This was eighteen adolescents, and strain was estimated from skin markers and ultrasound.

00:33:25.637 --> 00:33:35.912
The tirzepatide trial from the news segment belongs here too, as the clearest evidence this year that resistance training changes what a large weight loss does to strength.

00:33:42.092 --> 00:33:54.942
A rule question with wider reach. At Connecticut's high school cross-country State Open on the fifth of November, Quentin Koch of Xavier finished second and was disqualified for wearing a heart rate monitor on his arm.

00:33:54.942 --> 00:34:13.642
Marathon Handbook quotes the state association's rule, set in capitals: "ANY RUNNER WHO USES AN ILLEGAL AID OTHER THAN A WATCH, SUCH AS A HEART RATE MONITOR, SHALL BE DISQUALIFIED." The national federation's rules have allowed wearables since twenty nineteen and restrict only data sent to a runner during a race.

00:34:13.642 --> 00:34:30.117
As Marathon Handbook notes, a watch with an optical sensor collects much the same data as an arm band, and the rule does not say whether that watch is still just a watch. His coach said the band's data could be read only after the race, and that close to half the field wore something similar.

00:34:30.117 --> 00:34:41.992
Koch can wear the same band at the New England Championships on the fourteenth. The point beyond Connecticut is that rules written in terms of device types are aging badly when every watch is a sensor.

00:34:42.642 --> 00:34:57.942
On shoes, Doctors of Running reviewed three this week, and none qualifies for detail under this digest's standing focus on models made in a wide fitting and sold in the U K. The Nike Alphafly four has no wide version listed on Nike's U K site.

00:34:57.942 --> 00:35:17.017
The Altra Torin S T is sold in a men's two E width by American retailers, but I could not confirm a wide version on Altra's U K site or at U K retailers, which list the standard width. The K Swiss Echo Soft has no wide version that I could find, and Doctors of Running describe its toe box as tapered.

00:35:23.197 --> 00:35:34.472
A short, selective list of what else the sources published, by title and the publishers' own descriptions. I have not read or heard these in full, so this is what exists, not what it claims.

00:35:35.122 --> 00:35:52.672
On the podcasts. Doctors of Running episode three hundred and nineteen pits shoes against each other for scenarios from a marathon personal best to a first fifty kilometres. Sigma Nutrition episode six hundred and twenty-six is with Professor Charlyn Black on diet for healthy ageing.

00:35:52.672 --> 00:36:08.647
Iron Culture episode three hundred and ninety-five has Eric Helms and the researcher Nick Tiller on science communication against what they call commodification. The Physiology of Endurance Running looks at what running YouTubers say about thresholds and economy.

00:36:08.647 --> 00:36:25.172
That Triathlon Show's research roundup includes Arthur Bossi's reanalysis of Hickson's nineteen seventy-seven V O two max study, which this programme covered in episode six. The OMNIA Performance podcast is on building strength and endurance at the same time.

00:36:25.822 --> 00:36:45.422
In writing. On Steve Magness's Science of Running podcast, he and his co-host ask why track and field is hard to follow. The Morning Shakeout's issue five seventy-four covers the New York marathon. Marco Altini has rebuilt the H R V four Training app. D C Rainmaker is preparing a roundup of indoor trainer apps.

00:36:45.422 --> 00:37:03.297
Running Explained has an off-season nutrition piece with the sports dietitian Claire Shorenstein. NutritionFacts dot org has two posts on the rare sugar allulose. The Physicians Committee for Responsible Medicine, an advocacy group, published a release criticising the current U S dietary guidelines.

00:37:03.297 --> 00:37:23.122
Marathon Handbook ran a series of data pieces on the New York marathon, including an age-graded re-run of the race that a forty-six-year-old won, and reported that Stephanie Case became the first woman to run a hundred miles in Antarctica. And Retraction Watch reports that misconduct cases in South Korea are taking years to resolve.

00:37:29.312 --> 00:37:46.962
That is episode eleven. The open items for next week: whether the Santa Barbara police investigation finds anything beyond heat, how the Oketch case proceeds, and whether Peter Attia sets out his revised view on zone two in his own words. The companion notes carry every source.
